ldm is the LTSP Display Manager which handles authentication for remote desktop clients.

Currently, it doesn't interface with a greeter, as this design will require a
conversation with the greeter, rather than a simple "get name and password"
of the existing greeter.

Work on the greeter will also be done.

For now, We're using a simple GTK greeter that Ollie designed.  We'll make
it more pretty.

If you'd like to play with ldm, here's the steps.

1) Get the branch (you've obviously done this already)
2) Build ldm (standard ./configure --prefix=usr/local && make)
3) Copy ldm to $CHROOT/usr/sbin
4) Copy share/ldm-script to $CHROOT/usr/share/ldm
5) Copy scripts/* to $CHROOT/usr/share/ldm/rc.d
6) Install ldmgtkgreet to $CHROOT/usr/libexec/ldm

Currently, most things work.  Sound and Localdev seems to setup ok.
The command socket for ldm now lives in  /var/run/ldm_socket, snd the udev
scripts have been patched up in my ltspfs-upstream branch.

LDM_DIRECTX works!

We've got a new scripting mechanism for startup and shutdown scripts.
Right after the ssh connections been completed,
"/usr/share/ldm/ldm-script start" is called, which exectutes everything
called S* in the rc.d dir in the ldm share.

Two environment variables are passed down:

LDM_SERVER points to the server LDM is talking to, and
LDM_SOCKET points to the command socket the ssh connection has set up.

A three line script now replaces all the functionality of the delayed-mounter
daemon.

to exectute something on the server, you can simply do:

ssh -M $LDM_SOCKET $LDM_SERVER "your server command here"

After the X session's ended, ldm-script stop is called which looks for K*
scripts.

Note that the scripts are .'d so, exits in your rc scripts will be bad mojo.

Have Fun!

--
The LTSP Team
http://www.ltsp.org


===============================================================================
Building and packaging this fork (Debian 13 "Trixie")
===============================================================================

This is a fork of the last upstream ldm release (2:2.18.06, last packaged for
Debian Buster) updated to build and run on current Debian: gtkgreet has been
ported from GTK2 (removed from Debian's archive) to GTK3, an xfreerdp backend
plugin was added alongside the original ssh one, and a number of security and
correctness fixes were made (see debian/changelog for the full list). The
version was bumped to 3.0.0 to reflect the GTK2->GTK3 switch, which is a
breaking change for anyone still trying to build the old 2.18.06 tree.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
1) Building from source, without packaging
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Install the build dependencies:

    sudo apt install build-essential autoconf automake libtool intltool \
        pkg-config libglib2.0-dev libgtk-3-dev libx11-dev libxext-dev \
        gettext iso-codes

Then, from the top of the source tree:

    ./autogen.sh
    ./configure --prefix=/usr
    make -j$(nproc)
    sudo make install

`./autogen.sh` regenerates the autotools files (configure, Makefile.in, ...)
from configure.ac/Makefile.am; skip it if you're building from a tarball
that already has a `configure` script.

If your checkout came from a Windows git client, the shell scripts may have
CRLF line endings, which breaks their shebang line ("/bin/sh -v\r" isn't a
valid interpreter). Fix that first with:

    find . -name .git -prune -o -type f \
        \( -iname '*.png' -o -iname '*.jpg' \) -prune -o -type f -print \
        | xargs dos2unix

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
2) Building an installable .deb package (recommended)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is the normal Debian packaging workflow, using the debian/ directory
in this tree - no separate packaging repo needed.

Install the packaging + build dependencies (or let step 3 below do it for
you):

    sudo apt install build-essential debhelper debhelper-compat dpkg-dev \
        autoconf automake libtool intltool pkg-config dctrl-tools \
        libglib2.0-dev libgtk-3-dev libx11-dev libxext-dev \
        gettext iso-codes locales python3

Then, from the top of the source tree:

    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

`-us -uc` skips GPG-signing the resulting .changes/.dsc files, which you
don't need for a purely local build. `-b` builds binary packages only (no
source package). This produces, one directory *above* the source tree:

    ldm_3.0.0-1_amd64.deb        - the client (ldm core, gtkgreet, wwm,
                                    ssh + xfreerdp plugins, ldm-dialog)
    ldm-dbgsym_3.0.0-1_amd64.deb - debug symbols (safe to ignore/skip)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
3) Installing the .deb
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

Use `apt install ./<file>.deb` (not `dpkg -i`) so apt resolves and pulls in
the runtime dependencies (openssh-client, xserver-xorg, etc.) automatically:

    sudo apt install ./ldm_3.0.0-1_amd64.deb \

For the xfreerdp backend specifically, also install (it's a Recommends,
not a hard dependency, since the ssh backend works without it):

    sudo apt install freerdp3-x11

(Debian 13 only ships freerdp3-x11, whose binary is `xfreerdp3`; the
xfreerdp plugin auto-detects this - see `RDP_XFREERDP_BIN` in lts.conf to
override the auto-detection if needed.)

To rebuild after making further source changes, just repeat step 2 - there's
no need to clean the tree first; dpkg-buildpackage reruns autoreconf/configure
each time.

